  "conclusions": {
    "constant_hazard": "The adjusted piecewise model does not reject a constant elapsed-time hazard at the 5% level (LRT p = 0.591). It rests on 123 observed repeats.",
    "low_then_rising": "181-365, 366-730 days vs 0-180 days (unadjusted): rate ratio 0.86 (95% CI 0.54-1.39), about 14% lower; two-sided p = 0.545. Not statistically significant at the 5% level: the data do not reject equal rates (rate ratio 1); the 95% CI runs from 0.54 to 1.39, which includes 1. The estimate is in the opposite direction to the hypothesis (lower, not higher). Repeat events: 48 in 181-365, 366-730 days and 26 in 0-180 days.",
    "u_shape": [
      "Directional U-shape test (all of: response vs immediate lower; long-term vs response higher), intersection p = 0.440. Not statistically significant at the 5% level: the data do not reject the null of no U-shape. The point estimates have the hypothesised shape. The weakest part is response vs immediate (p = 0.440).",
      "- response vs immediate: rate ratio 0.96 (95% CI 0.59-1.56), about 4% lower; one-sided p = 0.440 (alternative: lower). Not statistically significant at the 5% level: the data do not reject the null of no decrease (rate ratio at least 1); the 95% CI runs from 0.59 to 1.56, which includes 1. Repeat events: 47 in the response period and 26 in the immediate period.",
      "- long-term vs response: rate ratio 1.22 (95% CI 0.80-1.85), about 22% higher; one-sided p = 0.178 (alternative: higher). Not statistically significant at the 5% level: the data do not reject the null of no increase (rate ratio at most 1); the 95% CI runs from 0.80 to 1.85, which includes 1. Repeat events: 48 in the long-term period and 47 in the response period.",
      "- long-term vs immediate: rate ratio 1.17 (95% CI 0.71-1.93), about 17% higher; two-sided p = 0.533. Not statistically significant at the 5% level: the data do not reject equal rates (rate ratio 1); the 95% CI runs from 0.71 to 1.93, which includes 1. Repeat events: 48 in the long-term period and 26 in the immediate period."
    ],
    "delayed_peak_sensitivity": [
      "Directional delayed peak test (all of: 91-180 vs 0-90 higher; 91-180 vs post-180 higher), intersection p = 0.486. Not statistically significant at the 5% level: the data do not reject the null of no delayed peak. The point estimates have the hypothesised shape. The weakest part is 91-180 vs post-180 (p = 0.486).",
      "- 91-180 vs 0-90: rate ratio 1.12 (95% CI 0.52-2.41), about 12% higher; one-sided p = 0.388 (alternative: higher). Not statistically significant at the 5% level: the data do not reject the null of no increase (rate ratio at most 1); the 95% CI runs from 0.52 to 2.41, which includes 1. Repeat events: 13 in 91-180 days and 13 in 0-90 days.",
      "- 91-180 vs post-180: rate ratio 1.01 (95% CI 0.56-1.82), about 1% higher; one-sided p = 0.486 (alternative: higher). Not statistically significant at the 5% level: the data do not reject the null of no increase (rate ratio at most 1); the 95% CI runs from 0.56 to 1.82, which includes 1. Repeat events: 13 in 91-180 days and 95 in post-180 days.",
